{
  "$schema": "https://docs.oasis-open.org/sarif/sarif/v2.1.0/errata01/os/schemas/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": { "driver": { "name": "pmat" } },
      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "f048_still_fails_on_nonfinite_output",
          "level": "note",
          "message": { "text": "RmsNormBrick::run (crates/aprender-serve/src/brick/brick_impls.rs:58-88, included by mod.rs:439) builds the output, then runs every assertion from assertions(): no_nan, no_inf, budget_met, through AssertionKind::check_f32 (mod.rs:299-338). A NaN or Inf output returns Err(AssertionFailed), so brick.run(..).expect(\"operation failed\") panics in the test; the test's own is_nan and is_finite asserts are unchanged and also fail. The delta removes only the wall-time budget." },
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "measured",
            "command": ["pmat", "query", "RmsNormBrick run", "--include-source", "--limit", "3"],
            "exit_code": 0,
            "stdout_sha256": "not_recorded",
            "failure_scenario": "an eps change that makes rms zero gives NaN or Inf output; run returns Err and the test fails",
            "precision_class": "advisory"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "removed_budget_hides_nothing_the_test_claims",
          "level": "note",
          "message": { "text": "The test is named f048_rmsnorm_epsilon and its comment says it checks that epsilon prevents division by zero on near-zero input. That property is the finiteness of the output, still asserted three ways (run's no_nan/no_inf, then the two test asserts). The 1 ms budget was a latency check on a loaded runner, which the name does not claim. Latency stays covered by the other brick tests that keep real budgets, e.g. rmsnorm_brick_runs in tests_f001_brick_f002.rs:143." },
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "asserted",
            "rationale": "read of the test at tests_r006_fused.rs:327-346 and of RmsNormBrick::run",
            "failure_scenario": "none found; a latency regression in RmsNorm is not this test's job",
            "precision_class": "advisory"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "f64_max_budget_no_overflow_or_nan",
          "level": "note",
          "message": { "text": "TokenBudget::from_latency(f64::MAX) (mod.rs:60) stores us_per_token = f64::MAX and tokens_per_sec = 1_000_000.0 / f64::MAX, about 5.6e-303: a finite positive normal value, no underflow to zero, no NaN. TokenResult::new computes budget.is_met(us_per_token), which is actual <= f64::MAX: always true for a finite actual (elapsed microseconds cast from u128 to f64). No arithmetic on f64::MAX that could overflow occurs on this path; gap_factor is actual / MAX, which is finite and not called here. budget_met is therefore true and the budget_met assertion passes." },
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "asserted",
            "rationale": "read of from_latency, is_met, gap_factor and TokenResult::new",
            "failure_scenario": "none: only NaN actual or a NaN budget would make the comparison false, neither is reachable",
            "precision_class": "advisory"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "delta_scope_one_test_only",
          "level": "note",
          "message": { "text": "git diff ab53111dfc 7f4fca56c4 touches only crates/aprender-serve/src/brick/tests_r006_fused.rs: one comment line and one expression in f048_rmsnorm_epsilon. No production code, no other test, no workflow or manifest. The comment says FLAKE-0, which matches the fleet rule on flaky tests. Nothing else in the delta." },
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "measured",
            "command": ["git", "diff", "ab53111dfc", "7f4fca56c4"],
            "exit_code": 0,
            "stdout_sha256": "not_recorded",
            "failure_scenario": "none",
            "precision_class": "advisory"
          }
        }
      ]
